Approach:

The site is divided into 2 parts and on completing one part a flag is generated. The 1st part consisted of 
7 mcq type questions only correctly answering them the 1st flag was available. These questions consisted of 
the different types of attacks that can happen and how to overcome these attacks.

The second part had 3 scenarions here we have to correctly guess where we have to put the sheild in order to
prevent the data breach.

a. Prompt injection: A prompt injection attack is incoming. The attacker will attempt to override system instructions
                     by embedding malicious commands in the user input. You have 2 shields — place them wisely.

            User Input---->API Gateway(routes request)---->Prompt(assembled prompt)---->LLM agent(fetch context/context)---->retrival(query)---->database(results)

here we place the sheild at Prompt cause this is where the input is incorporated and LLM agent as it receives final 
prompt and if it receives tampered instructions it will expose data.

b. Sensetive data leakage attack: A data leakage attack is incoming. The attacker will attempt to extract sensitive information
                                  through crafted queries. You have 3 shields — protect the components that handle data.

           User Input---->API Gateway(routes request)---->Prompt(assembled prompt)---->LLM agent(fetch context/context)---->retrival(query)---->database(results)

here we palce the sheild at "LLM" as it decides what to include in the response, "retrival" as it fetches contextual data. 
If filtering is weak, it may return sensitive information. and database cause it stores embeddings or records that may 
contain confidential data. 

c. Data poisoning: A data poisoning attack is incoming. The attacker has injected malicious data that will propagate through
the system from the data layer. You have 2 shields — protect the data components.

         User Input---->API Gateway(routes request)---->Prompt(assembled prompt)---->LLM agent(fetch context/context)---->retrival(query)---->database(results)

here we place the sheild at "retrival" cause if poisoned data is stored and later retrieved, it influences model outputs even
after deployment. And "database" because it stores training or behavioral data.

